# Expectation record for 03_initializers/011_flexible_array_member.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 011_flexible_array_member
area               = 03_initializers
description        = Flexible array member declaration and sizing through allocated-equivalent storage at BOTH storage durations: a union pairing the incomplete-array struct with a same-prefix sized struct, instantiated once as a file-scope static image and once as an automatic twin inside main, with the shared count written through one arm and read back through the other and across a function boundary that knows only the incomplete type, and the element data carried by the sized arm where it is declared
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour, which is the precondition both
differential oracles rest on: undefined behaviour would license either compiler to do anything at
all, so a divergence between two compilers over it would prove nothing about either one. The storage
technique below was chosen precisely to keep that true.
THE USUAL HEAP IDIOM IS UNAVAILABLE, AND ITS ABSENCE COSTS NO COVERAGE. Populating a flexible array
member is normally done with an allocation sized as the fixed header plus n elements; here no header
may be included, so no allocator is even declared, and the suite is hermetic, so no dynamic
allocation is performed at all. The storage is an allocated-equivalent object instead -- once with
STATIC storage duration at file scope and once with AUTOMATIC storage duration inside main -- and
both exercise the same access paths.
ALIASING IS THE HAZARD IN THIS CONSTRUCT, AND IT IS MADE LEGAL BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN BY HOPE.
A bare `static int backing[N]` cast to `struct fam *` WOULD be a strict-aliasing violation -- the
storage would be written as an int array and read through an unrelated struct type -- so that
spelling is not used. Instead both types are members of the SAME union: C11 6.5.2.3p6 permits
inspecting the common initial sequence of structures that share a union, which here is the leading
`int count`, and the two data arrays begin at the same offset with the same element type, so every
access reads or writes storage last written through an lvalue of that same type. No pointer cast
appears anywhere in the program.
The flexible array member itself contributes nothing to sizeof the struct (C11 6.7.2.1p18), which is
asserted directly rather than relied on silently: sizeof_struct_fam is printed and must read 4, and
fam_size_as_if_omitted asserts the inequality the standard actually states -- the size is as if the
member were omitted, allowing only extra trailing padding -- against a header-only twin type.
NO SUBSCRIPT IS EVER APPLIED TO THE FLEXIBLE MEMBER, AND THAT IS THE PROGRAM'S CENTRAL SAFETY
PROPERTY. C11 6.7.2.1p18 replaces the member with the longest array that would not make the structure
larger than THE OBJECT BEING ACCESSED, and states that if that array would have no elements, any
attempt to access an element is undefined. A `struct fam *` aimed at the union's flexible arm
designates an object of size sizeof(struct fam) -- four bytes, the header alone -- so the replacement
array there has NO elements and subscripting it would be out of bounds on the read side and on the
write side alike. The union makes the two arms alias legitimately; it does not enlarge either arm.
Every element access in this program is therefore made through the SIZED arm, whose data member is
declared with FAM_CAP == 8 elements, and the flexible arm is used only for the member the two share.
EVERY SUBSCRIPT IS IN BOUNDS, AND EVERY BOUND IS DERIVED FROM THE OBJECT RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
sum_prefix loops to u->flex.count -- the shared member, read through the flexible arm -- and indexes
u->sized.data, so the bound comes from the object and the storage comes from the arm that declares it.
The static object's count is only ever 5 then 6 and the highest index it touches is 5; the automatic
object's count is only ever 4 then 5 and the highest index it touches is 4. All four counts are
strictly below the capacity, so no access reaches past the backing array, no one-past-the-end pointer
is formed or dereferenced, and no pointer arithmetic leaves its object. `p` designates the file-scope
object, which lives for the whole run, and `lp` designates the automatic object, which is read only
inside main where it is alive, so neither pointer is ever dangling.
NO UNINITIALIZED STORAGE IS READ, at either storage duration. Both unions carry a designated
initializer for their sized arm, so every element of that arm is determinate -- the live values plus
the explicit trailing zeros -- and a member or element an initializer had omitted would be zeroed in
any case, for automatic and static duration alike (C11 6.7.9p19 and p21). The static object is
additionally covered by C11 6.7.9p10, which defines a static-duration object without an initializer as
zero, but the automatic twin does not rely on any such pre-zeroing: its whole image is established by
emitted stores on entry to the block, which is the very difference the twin exists to test. In each
half, the one slot the mutation phase newly brings inside the count -- index 5 in the static object and
index 4 in the automatic one -- is written before it is read.
No signed overflow is possible: the largest printed value is 2133 and the largest element is 600,
while INT_MAX is at least 2147483647 on every conforming target. There is no shift, no division, no
remainder and no integer-to-pointer conversion anywhere in the program.
No object is modified twice between two sequence points. Each mutation phase is three separate
statements -- one element rewritten in place through the sized arm, the count grown by one through the
flexible arm, one previously zero slot filled through the sized arm -- and the accumulation inside
sum_prefix is a statement of its own. Every printf call receives
at most one loop counter and one already-computed value, so the unspecified order in which a function
call's arguments are evaluated cannot be observed.
No object representation is inspected and nothing is read byte by byte, so neither padding nor byte
order can influence the output, and no address is printed -- the two views are reached only through
int-typed lvalues at identical offsets. Nothing depends on the relative addresses of unrelated
objects: no pointer difference is taken and no two addresses are compared.
Every conversion specifier is %d and every corresponding argument has type int after the default
argument promotions -- including both sizeof results, each cast to int before reaching the variadic
call so that no wider type and no size_t is passed -- so no variadic argument is read at the wrong
width.
main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is truncated by the
operating system. Every input is a literal in this file: nothing is read from the environment, the
clock, the file system or the network, and iteration order is fixed by counts the program itself
printed, so every cell is reproducible from this source and this record alone.
No header is included anywhere and printf is declared by hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`.
bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a bonus stdatomic.h and ships no standard
I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so naming one would fail on the bcc side of oracle (a)
while succeeding on the reference side -- a spurious divergence caused by the test rather than by a
compiler. This area is granted no header exception; the corpus has exactly two, area 07's stdarg.h
and the dedicated bundled-header probe in area 12.
This record declares no warning-gate deviation, and area 03 sanctions none. The program is clean
under the full default gate -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow
-Werror at -O0, -O1 and -O2 with all four reference drivers, and it builds and runs clean under the
sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all. Both gates are
reference-compiler-only and are never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline is
untouched.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows two things and records the reason for each below, as the suite requires: one
SPELLING of the initializer, and one unsound WAY OF REACHING the member's elements. It narrows no
target, no optimization level and no oracle, it carries no expected-divergence marker, and no
`ub_audit_flags` key appears because the program passes the suite's full default warning gate
unmodified.
ELEMENT ACCESS THROUGH THE FLEXIBLE MEMBER ITSELF IS EXCLUDED, AND THE REASON IS THE STANDARD RATHER
THAN DIFFICULTY. An earlier form of this program subscripted the member through a `struct fam *`
aimed at the union arm. Under C11 6.7.2.1p18 the replacement array for that access is sized against
the object being accessed, which is the four-byte `struct fam` arm, so it has no elements and every
such subscript was undefined -- exactly the kind of unsound input that would make any divergence it
provoked unattributable. The construction the standard unambiguously supports is an allocated object
genuinely larger than the header, and it is out of reach for this corpus: a portable declaration of an
allocation function needs size_t, which is `unsigned int` on i686 and `unsigned long` on the other
three targets, so no single spelling serves all four; the corpus forbids including the header that
would define it; and a declaration without a prototype was MEASURED to be rejected by the reference
compiler and by the alternate reference compiler alike under the mandatory gate
(-Werror=builtin-declaration-mismatch and -Werror=strict-prototypes respectively). The exclusion is
therefore narrow -- one way of reaching one member -- and it costs no coverage of the feature that can
be observed soundly: the declaration is accepted, the sizeof contribution is measured twice over, the
incomplete type crosses a function boundary, and the member the two arms share is written through one
arm and read through the other, which is precisely the access C11 6.5.2.3p6 sanctions. The elements
themselves are carried by the sized arm, where they are declared and where subscripting them is
beyond argument. Per constraint C3 this is stated rather than left silent, in the program's own
header comment as well as here.
ONE SPELLING OF THIS CONSTRUCT IS EXCLUDED, NARROWLY AND ON AN EXPLICIT PLAN BASIS RATHER THAN ON
DIFFICULTY. Direct initialization -- `static struct fam d = { 3, {1,2,3} };` -- was probed against
the mandatory strict gate in this environment and REJECTED with "error: initialization of a flexible
array member" under -Werror=pedantic. Unlike every other gate interaction in this feature area, this
one is NOT a style diagnostic reporting a well-defined construct: initializing a flexible array
member is a GNU EXTENSION rather than standard C, which is exactly why -pedantic -- whose entire
purpose is to reject extensions -- diagnoses it. It is the one place in this area where the gate is
reporting a real portability fact.
THE BASIS FOR EXCLUDING IT is the plan's own specification for this program, which PRESCRIBES the
technique to use, naming "flexible array member declaration, sizing, and access through
allocated-equivalent static storage", and which assigns GNU extensions to the 08_gcc_extensions
area, whose fixed program list this construct is not on. Area 03 sanctions no gate deviation, and
inventing one to admit a non-standard spelling would weaken requirement 1's machine half for every
other program in the area. Suppressing -pedantic here with a scoped pragma -- the mechanism used
elsewhere in this area for genuinely standard constructs -- would not merely relax a style check: it
would move the construct under test from standard C to a GNU extension while leaving it in an area
that does not test extensions, so the divergence surface would no longer be the one this program is
named for.
THE EXCLUSION IS SCOPED TO ONE WAY OF FILLING THE MEMBER AND COSTS NO COVERAGE OF THE FEATURE, and
it is recorded here rather than left to look like an oversight. Declaration of the incomplete last
member, its zero contribution to sizeof, access through the incomplete type across a function
boundary where the callee must take the element count from the object itself, and mutation through
both union arms are all exercised -- and now at BOTH storage durations, since g_store is a
data-section image and l_store is materialized by emitted stores on entry to main. A compiler can get
the static image right and the run-time stores wrong, so both paths are held side by side. The union
technique that appears instead was probed the same way and is gate-clean at -O0, -O1 and -O2 as well
as sanitizer-clean, and it is the better answer on its own merits rather than merely the one that
compiles, because it makes the aliasing legitimate by construction.
WIDTH NORMALIZATION. Every value is an int printed with %d, and both printed sizes are cast to int
before they reach the variadic call, so no size_t is ever passed. sizeof is never applied to a
pointer, and no long, ptrdiff_t, pointer, floating or character value appears anywhere, so neither
the MEASURED pointer width -- four bytes on i686 and eight on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64
(docs/technical-specifications.md target table, lines 457-462) -- nor the MEASURED plain-char
signedness split, signed on x86_64 and i686 and unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64, can influence a byte
of stdout.
SIZES ARE TARGET-INVARIANT FOR A REASON THAT WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. int is 4 bytes and
4-byte aligned on all four targets, so no padding sits between count and data in either union arm:
sizeof(struct fam) is 4, because the flexible array member contributes nothing to the size of the
struct (C11 6.7.2.1p18), and sizeof(union fam_storage) is 4 + 8 * 4 == 36, the size of its sized arm.
PADDING, BYTE ORDER AND ADDRESSES. No object representation is inspected and nothing is read byte by
byte, so neither padding nor endianness can influence the output, and no address is ever printed --
the two views are reached only through int-typed lvalues at identical offsets.
EXECUTION CHARACTER SET. No character data appears anywhere, so C11 5.2.1 leaves nothing here to the
implementation.
THE TWO STORAGE DURATIONS ARE THIS PROGRAM'S VARIANT AXIS. The file-scope union is a translation-time
data-section image, while its automatic twin -- identical union, identical designated initializer --
must be materialized by emitted stores on entry to the block, so the same syntax travels two
different paths through the compiler. Both are compiled at all three optimization levels, and no
volatile operand is added: qualifying one would suppress exactly the optimizer behaviour the pairing
exists to survive.
MEASURED EVIDENCE. The program was compiled with the reference driver for all four targets at -O0,
-O1 and -O2 -- twelve configurations -- and every one produced these 35 lines byte-identically with
exit status 0, which is why oracle (b) compares this program's targets against each other without any
narrowing and why oracle (c) can hold these bytes as the golden record. Every live element is printed
individually in both the read-back and the post-mutation phase of both halves, so a single wrong slot
localizes to one initializer form at one storage duration rather than to the aggregate, and the four
sum lines, together with count_arms_agree and the three count_via_boundary lines, confirm that the
same storage is reached through both union arms rather than merely that each arm is self-consistent.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
sizeof_struct_fam=4
sizeof_storage=36
fam_size_as_if_omitted=1
count=5
count_arms_agree=1
count_via_boundary=5
data[0]=100
data[1]=200
data[2]=300
data[3]=400
data[4]=500
sum=1500
after_count=6
after_count_via_boundary=6
after[0]=100
after[1]=200
after[2]=333
after[3]=400
after[4]=500
after[5]=600
after_sum=2133
l_count=4
l_count_via_boundary=4
l_data[0]=11
l_data[1]=22
l_data[2]=33
l_data[3]=44
l_sum=110
l_after_count=5
l_after[0]=11
l_after[1]=99
l_after[2]=33
l_after[3]=44
l_after[4]=55
l_after_sum=242
END
